/* Verse Games: THE WHOLE COUNSEL · 1 Timothy chapters 1-6
   Verse text is filled/verified from the KJV by tools/fill-verses.js */
VG.registerBibleChapters("1timothy", [
{n:1, name:"The Chief of Sinners",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 1:15",verse:"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief."},
 facts:["Did you know Paul's greeting to Timothy carries three gifts instead of the usual two: 'grace, mercy, and peace'? Mercy appears in all three Pastoral Epistles, written to men holding hard assignments (1 Timothy 1:2).","Did you know 'of whom I am chief' was not false modesty? Paul calls his own conversion a pattern of Christ's longsuffering, proof that no one is beyond saving (1 Timothy 1:15-16).","Did you know the 'endless genealogies' troubling Ephesus were likely Jewish speculations about Old Testament family lines, spun into arguments instead of godliness (1 Timothy 1:4)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"Whom does Paul call 'our hope' in his greeting?", options:["The Lord Jesus Christ","Timothy","The church at Ephesus","The apostles"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:1",verse:"Paul, an apostle of Jesus Christ by the commandment of God our Saviour, and Lord Jesus Christ, which is our hope;", insight:"Hope has a name in this letter; it is a person, not a prospect."},
{q:"How does Paul describe Timothy in his greeting?", options:["My own son in the faith","My fellow apostle","My brother in bonds","My servant in the gospel"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:2",verse:"Unto Timothy, my own son in the faith: Grace, mercy, and peace, from God our Father and Jesus Christ our Lord.", insight:"Timothy was Paul's son in the faith; spiritual fatherhood was as real to Paul as blood."},
{q:"Where did Paul urge Timothy to stay when he went into Macedonia?", options:["At Ephesus","At Corinth","At Philippi","At Thessalonica"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:3",verse:"As I besought thee to abide still at Ephesus, when I went into Macedonia, that thou mightest charge some that they teach no other doctrine,", insight:"Timothy stayed at Ephesus while Paul moved on; faithfulness sometimes means holding the post, not taking the journey."},
{q:"What were the false teachers giving heed to?", options:["Fables and endless genealogies","The Roman laws","The Greek philosophers","The temple rituals"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:4",verse:"Neither give heed to fables and endless genealogies, which minister questions, rather than godly edifying which is in faith: so do.", insight:"Fables and endless genealogies minister questions, not godliness; some studies only make people argue."},
{q:"What three things does Paul say are the end of the commandment?", options:["Charity out of a pure heart, a good conscience, and faith unfeigned","Wealth, honor, and long life","Knowledge, power, and wisdom","Prayer, fasting, and alms"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:5",verse:"Now the end of the commandment is charity out of a pure heart, and of a good conscience, and of faith unfeigned:", insight:"The goal of all commandment is love from a pure heart; any teaching that does not end in love has missed its mark."},
{q:"Unto what had some swerved and turned aside?", options:["Unto vain jangling","Unto the worship of angels","Unto the keeping of feasts","Unto the service of Caesar"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:6",verse:"From which some having swerved have turned aside unto vain jangling;", insight:"Swerve a little from love and you land in vain jangling; small departures make noisy teachers."},
{q:"What did the would-be teachers of the law fail to understand?", options:["Neither what they say, nor whereof they affirm","The Greek language","The Roman law","The history of Israel"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:7",verse:"Desiring to be teachers of the law; understanding neither what they say, nor whereof they affirm.", insight:"They wanted the title of teacher without the understanding; confidence is no substitute for comprehension."},
{q:"When is the law good, according to Paul?", options:["If a man use it lawfully","If it is written on stone","If it is taught by priests","If it is kept perfectly"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:8",verse:"But we know that the law is good, if a man use it lawfully;", insight:"The law is good when used lawfully; even good tools harm in careless hands."},
{q:"For whom is the law made?", options:["For the lawless and disobedient","For the righteous man","For the angels","For the priests only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:9",verse:"Knowing this, that the law is not made for a righteous man, but for the lawless and disobedient, for the ungodly and for sinners, for unholy and profane, for murderers of fathers and murderers of mothers, for manslayers,", insight:"The law was not made for the righteous but for the lawless; medicine is for the sick, not the well."},
{q:"Whom does Paul name among those the law confronts?", options:["Liars, and perjured persons","The poor and the stranger","Widows and orphans","Children and servants"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:10",verse:"For whoremongers, for them that defile themselves with mankind, for menstealers, for liars, for perjured persons, and if there be any other thing that is contrary to sound doctrine;", insight:"Liars and perjured persons stand in Paul's list; God takes truth on the lips seriously."},
{q:"What was committed to Paul's trust?", options:["The glorious gospel of the blessed God","The treasury of the church","The letters of the apostles","The care of the widows"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:11",verse:"According to the glorious gospel of the blessed God, which was committed to my trust.", insight:"The gospel was committed to Paul's trust; the greatest treasure travels in earthen vessels."},
{q:"Why does Paul thank Christ Jesus?", options:["He counted me faithful, putting me into the ministry","He made me rich","He freed me from prison","He gave me a long life"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:12",verse:"And I thank Christ Jesus our Lord, who hath enabled me, for that he counted me faithful, putting me into the ministry;", insight:"Christ counted Paul faithful and put him into the ministry; God trusts the forgiven with His work."},
{q:"What had Paul been before mercy found him?", options:["A blasphemer, and a persecutor, and injurious","A tax collector","A Roman soldier","A temple guard"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:13",verse:"Who was before a blasphemer, and a persecutor, and injurious: but I obtained mercy, because I did it ignorantly in unbelief.", insight:"Blasphemer, persecutor, injurious; no past disqualifies a man when mercy is this abundant, and he did it ignorantly in unbelief."},
{q:"What was exceeding abundant toward Paul?", options:["The grace of our Lord, with faith and love","The wrath of God","The riches of this world","The praises of men"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:14",verse:"And the grace of our Lord was exceeding abundant with faith and love which is in Christ Jesus.", insight:"Grace was exceeding abundant, with faith and love; where sin was chief, grace was more."},
{q:"What is the faithful saying, worthy of all acceptation?", options:["Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief","Blessed are the peacemakers","The Lord is my shepherd","Be strong and of good courage"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:15",verse:"This is a faithful saying, and worthy of all acceptation, that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners; of whom I am chief.", insight:"Paul names himself the chief of sinners so that every sinner after him can hope; the worst past makes the brightest pattern."},
{q:"Why did Paul obtain mercy?", options:["That Christ might shew forth all longsuffering, for a pattern to believers","That he might become rich","That he might rule the church","That he might write many books"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:16",verse:"Howbeit for this cause I obtained mercy, that in me first Jesus Christ might shew forth all longsuffering, for a pattern to them which should hereafter believe on him to life everlasting.", insight:"Paul's mercy was a pattern for all who would believe after him; your rescue is someone else's roadmap."},
{q:"How does Paul describe God in his burst of praise?", options:["The King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God","The God of war and thunder","The silent and distant God","The God of the hills only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:17",verse:"Now unto the King eternal, immortal, invisible, the only wise God, be honour and glory for ever and ever. Amen.", insight:"King eternal, immortal, invisible; praise remembers who God is before asking what He will do."},
{q:"What charge does Paul commit to Timothy?", options:["That thou mightest war a good warfare","That thou mightest build a temple","That thou mightest sail to Rome","That thou mightest gather gold"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:18",verse:"This charge I commit unto thee, son Timothy, according to the prophecies which went before on thee, that thou by them mightest war a good warfare;", insight:"War a good warfare; ministry is a battle, and prophecies once spoken become weapons in it."},
{q:"What happened to those who put away faith and a good conscience?", options:["They made shipwreck concerning faith","They became rich","They were praised by all","They found peace"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:19",verse:"Holding faith, and a good conscience; which some having put away concerning faith have made shipwreck:", insight:"Faith and a good conscience hold the ship together; cast them overboard and the wreck follows."},
{q:"Which two men did Paul deliver unto Satan?", options:["Hymenaeus and Alexander","Demas and Diotrephes","Phygellus and Hermogenes","Ananias and Sapphira"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 1:20",verse:"Of whom is Hymenaeus and Alexander; whom I have delivered unto Satan, that they may learn not to blaspheme.", insight:"Hymenaeus and Alexander were delivered unto Satan, that they might learn not to blaspheme; even discipline aims at learning."}
]},
{n:2, name:"Prayer for All Men",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 2:5",verse:"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus;"},
 facts:["Did you know Paul commands prayer for kings while Nero, who would one day have Paul executed, sat on the throne? The church prayed for its persecutors from the start (1 Timothy 2:2).","Did you know 'one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus' rules out every other go-between? No priest, angel, or saint shares that office (1 Timothy 2:5).","Did you know 'lifting up holy hands' pictures open, clean hands raised in surrender, the opposite of hands clenched in wrath (1 Timothy 2:8)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What does Paul urge to be made 'first of all'?", options:["Supplications, prayers, intercessions, and giving of thanks","Offerings of gold","Songs of triumph","Vows of silence"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:1",verse:"I exhort therefore, that, first of all, supplications, prayers, intercessions, and giving of thanks, be made for all men;", insight:"Prayer comes first of all, before plans and programs; the church's first work is on its knees."},
{q:"For whom are these prayers to be made?", options:["For all men","For the apostles only","For the Jews only","For the rich only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:1",verse:"I exhort therefore, that, first of all, supplications, prayers, intercessions, and giving of thanks, be made for all men;", insight:"For all men leaves no one outside the church's prayers; the gospel's reach sets prayer's range."},
{q:"For whom especially should we pray?", options:["For kings, and for all that are in authority","For the priests only","For the soldiers only","For the merchants only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:2",verse:"For kings, and for all that are in authority; that we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and honesty.", insight:"Paul bids prayer for kings, likely the hostile Nero himself; pray for rulers even when you would not vote for them."},
{q:"Why pray for those in authority?", options:["That we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and honesty","That they may become rich","That they may leave us alone","That they may fear us"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:2",verse:"For kings, and for all that are in authority; that we may lead a quiet and peaceable life in all godliness and honesty.", insight:"The goal is a quiet and peaceable life in godliness; good government is a gift that lets the gospel breathe."},
{q:"How does God view such prayer?", options:["It is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Saviour","It is a waste of time","It is only for priests","It is a last resort"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:3",verse:"For this is good and acceptable in the sight of God our Saviour;", insight:"Prayer for all is good and acceptable to God our Saviour; what pleases Him should fill our hours."},
{q:"What does God will for all men?", options:["To be saved, and to come unto the knowledge of the truth","To be rich and healthy","To rule the nations","To keep the law perfectly"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:4",verse:"Who will have all men to be saved, and to come unto the knowledge of the truth.", insight:"God wills all men to be saved; His heart is wider than our invitations."},
{q:"Who is the one mediator between God and men?", options:["The man Christ Jesus","The high priest","The angels","The apostles"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:5",verse:"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus;", insight:"One God, one mediator; no other name stands between you and the Father."},
{q:"Between whom does Christ stand as mediator?", options:["Between God and men","Between angels and men","Between Jew and Gentile only","Between heaven and earth only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:5",verse:"For there is one God, and one mediator between God and men, the man Christ Jesus;", insight:"He mediates between God and men because He is both; the man Christ Jesus bridges what sin divided."},
{q:"What did Christ give for all?", options:["Himself a ransom for all","Gold and silver","A new law","An earthly kingdom"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:6",verse:"Who gave himself a ransom for all, to be testified in due time.", insight:"He gave Himself a ransom for all; redemption's price was a person, not a payment."},
{q:"To what three roles was Paul ordained?", options:["A preacher, an apostle, and a teacher of the Gentiles","A priest, a king, and a prophet","A deacon, an elder, and a bishop","A scribe, a lawyer, and a judge"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:7",verse:"Whereunto I am ordained a preacher, and an apostle, (I speak the truth in Christ, and lie not;) a teacher of the Gentiles in faith and verity.", insight:"Preacher, apostle, teacher of the Gentiles; Paul's calling named his audience, and yours does too."},
{q:"How does Paul want men to pray?", options:["Lifting up holy hands, without wrath and doubting","With loud shouting only","With covered heads","In the temple only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:8",verse:"I will therefore that men pray every where, lifting up holy hands, without wrath and doubting.", insight:"Holy hands, without wrath and doubting; clenched fists and doubting hearts spoil prayer."},
{q:"Where does Paul want men to pray?", options:["Every where","In Jerusalem only","In the synagogue only","On the mountain only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:8",verse:"I will therefore that men pray every where, lifting up holy hands, without wrath and doubting.", insight:"Pray every where; no place is too common for communion with God."},
{q:"How should women adorn themselves?", options:["In modest apparel, with shamefacedness and sobriety","In the costliest array","With broided hair and much gold","As the fashion dictates"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:9",verse:"In like manner also, that women adorn themselves in modest apparel, with shamefacedness and sobriety; not with broided hair, or gold, or pearls, or costly array;", insight:"Modest apparel with shamefacedness and sobriety; godliness dresses for God's eyes first."},
{q:"Which of these does Paul list among unbecoming adornments?", options:["Broided hair","Simple linen","A wedding ring","A warm cloak"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:9",verse:"In like manner also, that women adorn themselves in modest apparel, with shamefacedness and sobriety; not with broided hair, or gold, or pearls, or costly array;", insight:"Broided hair, gold, pearls, costly array; when the wrapping outshines the gift, something is wrong."},
{q:"What becomes women professing godliness?", options:["Good works","Great wealth","Public praise","Many words"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:10",verse:"But (which becometh women professing godliness) with good works.", insight:"Good works become women professing godliness; the truest jewelry is a life of kindness."},
{q:"How is the woman to learn?", options:["In silence with all subjection","By teaching the men","In the marketplace","Through dreams only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:11",verse:"Let the woman learn in silence with all subjection.", insight:"Learn in silence with all subjection; a teachable spirit learns faster than a talking one."},
{q:"What does Paul not permit?", options:["A woman to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man","A woman to pray","A woman to give","A woman to sing"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:12",verse:"But I suffer not a woman to teach, nor to usurp authority over the man, but to be in silence.", insight:"This order guards the church's teaching office; roles differ, but worth before God does not."},
{q:"Who was formed first?", options:["Adam, then Eve","Eve, then Adam","Both together","Cain, then Abel"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:13",verse:"For Adam was first formed, then Eve.", insight:"Adam was first formed, then Eve; Paul grounds church order in creation, not in culture."},
{q:"Who was deceived and fell into transgression?", options:["The woman, not Adam","Adam, not the woman","Both equally at first","The serpent alone"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:14",verse:"And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression.", insight:"Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression; deception, not worth, is Paul's point."},
{q:"In what shall she be saved, continuing in faith, charity, holiness, and sobriety?", options:["In childbearing","In teaching","In ruling","In riches"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 2:15",verse:"Notwithstanding she shall be saved in childbearing, if they continue in faith and charity and holiness with sobriety.", insight:"Saved in childbearing, continuing in faith and holiness; God honors the quiet, faithful work of mothers."}
]},
{n:3, name:"Bishops and Deacons",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 3:16",verse:"And without controversy great is the mystery of godliness: God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory."},
 facts:["Did you know 'bishop' simply means overseer? Paul lists character, not charisma: blameless, vigilant, sober, hospitable, and apt to teach (1 Timothy 3:2).","Did you know deacons had to 'first be proved'? The early church watched a man's life before handing him the church's work (1 Timothy 3:10).","Did you know many scholars hear an early Christian hymn in 1 Timothy 3:16, six balanced lines tracing Christ from 'manifest in the flesh' to 'received up into glory'?"],
 questions:[
{q:"What does a man desire who desires the office of a bishop?", options:["A good work","Great riches","An easy life","High honor"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:1",verse:"This is a true saying, If a man desire the office of a bishop, he desireth a good work.", insight:"He desireth a good work; the office is labor to be loved, not a prize to be won."},
{q:"What must a bishop be in marriage?", options:["The husband of one wife","Unmarried","Married many times","A widower only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:2",verse:"A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach;", insight:"The husband of one wife; fidelity at home is the first credential for leading God's house."},
{q:"Which quality makes a bishop fit to instruct others?", options:["Apt to teach","Quick to argue","Slow to listen","Fond of stories"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:2",verse:"A bishop then must be blameless, the husband of one wife, vigilant, sober, of good behaviour, given to hospitality, apt to teach;", insight:"Apt to teach; a shepherd who cannot feed the flock starves it."},
{q:"What three things must a bishop avoid?", options:["Wine, striking, and greed for money","Meat, travel, and music","Sleep, rest, and silence","Books, letters, and scrolls"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:3",verse:"Not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre; but patient, not a brawler, not covetous;", insight:"Not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre; appetites, temper, and money disqualify faster than ignorance."},
{q:"What gentle quality must mark a bishop instead?", options:["Patient","Proud","Silent","Stern"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:3",verse:"Not given to wine, no striker, not greedy of filthy lucre; but patient, not a brawler, not covetous;", insight:"But patient; strength under control is the mark of a man fit to lead."},
{q:"How must a bishop rule his own house?", options:["Well, having his children in subjection with all gravity","Harshly, with an iron hand","Loosely, letting children rule","He need not rule it at all"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:4",verse:"One that ruleth well his own house, having his children in subjection with all gravity;", insight:"One that ruleth well his own house; the home is the proving ground for the church."},
{q:"What question does Paul ask about ruling the church?", options:["If a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?","Who will pay the church's debts?","Where shall the church meet?","When shall the church fast?"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:5",verse:"(For if a man know not how to rule his own house, how shall he take care of the church of God?)", insight:"How shall he take care of the church of God? Leadership at home is the rehearsal for leadership in the house of God."},
{q:"Why must a bishop not be a novice?", options:["Lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil","Lest he grow tired","Lest he ask too many questions","Lest he leave the city"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:6",verse:"Not a novice, lest being lifted up with pride he fall into the condemnation of the devil.", insight:"Not a novice, lest pride lift him up; new converts need roots before they carry weight."},
{q:"What reputation must a bishop have outside the church?", options:["A good report of them which are without","No reputation at all","A fearsome reputation","A wealthy reputation"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:7",verse:"Moreover he must have a good report of them which are without; lest he fall into reproach and the snare of the devil.", insight:"A good report of them which are without; the world watches, and a bad name becomes the devil's snare."},
{q:"How must deacons speak?", options:["Not doubletongued","With many words","In parables only","In whispers"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:8",verse:"Likewise must the deacons be grave, not doubletongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre;", insight:"Not doubletongued; a servant of the church must say the same thing to every face."},
{q:"What must deacons be in character?", options:["Grave","Merry at all times","Silent always","Bold in speech"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:8",verse:"Likewise must the deacons be grave, not doubletongued, not given to much wine, not greedy of filthy lucre;", insight:"Grave; dignity in deacons steadies the whole congregation."},
{q:"What must deacons hold?", options:["The mystery of the faith in a pure conscience","The church's money","The keys of the building","The bishop's letters"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:9",verse:"Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience.", insight:"Holding the mystery of the faith in a pure conscience; truth in the head needs cleanness in the heart."},
{q:"What must happen before deacons use the office?", options:["Let them first be proved","Let them pay a fee","Let them travel far","Let them be over fifty"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:10",verse:"And let these also first be proved; then let them use the office of a deacon, being found blameless.", insight:"First be proved, then serve; testing comes before trusting."},
{q:"What must the deacons' wives be?", options:["Grave, not slanderers, sober, faithful in all things","Wealthy and well dressed","Silent at all times","Keepers of the records"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:11",verse:"Even so must their wives be grave, not slanderers, sober, faithful in all things.", insight:"Not slanderers; the tongue of a leader's household can build or burn a church."},
{q:"How must deacons rule their homes?", options:["Ruling their children and their own houses well","By leaving all decisions to others","By hiring servants to rule","They are exempt from ruling"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:12",verse:"Let the deacons be the husbands of one wife, ruling their children and their own houses well.", insight:"Ruling their children and their own houses well; the deacon's home preaches before he opens his mouth."},
{q:"What do faithful deacons gain?", options:["A good degree, and great boldness in the faith","Gold and silver","A larger house","Freedom from work"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:13",verse:"For they that have used the office of a deacon well purchase to themselves a good degree, and great boldness in the faith which is in Christ Jesus.", insight:"A good degree and great boldness; faithful service in small things buys confidence for greater."},
{q:"What did Paul hope to do?", options:["To come unto Timothy shortly","To sail to Spain","To retire in peace","To write no more"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:14",verse:"These things write I unto thee, hoping to come unto thee shortly:", insight:"Hoping to come shortly; letters are good, but presence is better."},
{q:"What is the church of the living God called?", options:["The pillar and ground of the truth","The keeper of the gold","The mother of arts","The tower of strength"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:15",verse:"But if I tarry long, that thou mayest know how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth.", insight:"The pillar and ground of the truth; the church holds up God's truth the way pillars hold a roof."},
{q:"Who was manifest in the flesh?", options:["God","An angel","Moses","Elijah"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:16",verse:"And without controversy great is the mystery of godliness: God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory.", insight:"God was manifest in the flesh; the mystery of godliness is a person, Jesus Christ."},
{q:"Unto whom was He preached, according to the mystery of godliness?", options:["Unto the Gentiles","Only in Jerusalem","Only in the temple","In secret"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 3:16",verse:"And without controversy great is the mystery of godliness: God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory.", insight:"Preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world; the gospel was never meant to stay in one nation."}
]},
{n:4, name:"The Good Minister",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 4:16",verse:"Take heed unto thyself, and unto the doctrine; continue in them: for in doing this thou shalt both save thyself, and them that hear thee."},
 facts:["Did you know Paul says the Spirit 'speaketh expressly' about the last days? Some warnings in Scripture are whispered, but this one is announced (1 Timothy 4:1).","Did you know a 'seared' conscience pictures skin burned until it cannot feel? Paul warns that hypocrisy can deaden the very alarm God gave us (1 Timothy 4:2).","Did you know Timothy was young enough that Paul had to tell him, 'Let no man despise thy youth'? He may have been in his thirties, leading the church at Ephesus (1 Timothy 4:12)."],
 questions:[
{q:"What does the Spirit speak expressly about the latter times?", options:["Some shall depart from the faith","All men shall be saved","The temple shall be rebuilt","Wars shall cease"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:1",verse:"Now the Spirit speaketh expressly, that in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils;", insight:"The Spirit speaketh expressly; this warning is not a guess but a revelation."},
{q:"To what will they give heed?", options:["Seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils","The law of Moses","The wisdom of Greece","The songs of David"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:1",verse:"Now the Spirit speaketh expressly, that in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits, and doctrines of devils;", insight:"Seducing spirits and doctrines of devils; departure from the faith always has teachers."},
{q:"What is the conscience of these liars like?", options:["Seared with a hot iron","Soft as wax","Clean as snow","Sharp as a sword"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:2",verse:"Speaking lies in hypocrisy; having their conscience seared with a hot iron;", insight:"A conscience seared with a hot iron feels nothing; repeated sin burns out the alarm."},
{q:"What two things do they forbid or command?", options:["Forbidding to marry, and commanding to abstain from meats","Forbidding to pray, commanding to fast","Forbidding to sing, commanding silence","Forbidding to give, commanding to take"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:3",verse:"Forbidding to marry, and commanding to abstain from meats, which God hath created to be received with thanksgiving of them which believe and know the truth.", insight:"Forbidding to marry and commanding to abstain from meats; false holiness always adds rules God never gave."},
{q:"What does Paul say of every creature of God?", options:["It is good, and nothing to be refused, if received with thanksgiving","It is unclean","It is only for priests","It must be sacrificed"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:4",verse:"For every creature of God is good, and nothing to be refused, if it be received with thanksgiving:", insight:"Every creature of God is good; thanksgiving turns a meal into worship."},
{q:"By what two things is food sanctified?", options:["By the word of God and prayer","By salt and fire","By the priest's blessing only","By fasting first"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:5",verse:"For it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer.", insight:"Sanctified by the word of God and prayer; Scripture and prayer set the table apart."},
{q:"What makes a good minister of Jesus Christ?", options:["Being nourished up in the words of faith and of good doctrine","Being wealthy","Being well traveled","Being of noble birth"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:6",verse:"If thou put the brethren in remembrance of these things, thou shalt be a good minister of Jesus Christ, nourished up in the words of faith and of good doctrine, whereunto thou hast attained.", insight:"Nourished up in the words of faith; a minister feeds others from what first fed him."},
{q:"What should Timothy refuse?", options:["Profane and old wives' fables","The reading of Scripture","The counsel of elders","The prayers of the church"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:7",verse:"But refuse profane and old wives’ fables, and exercise thyself rather unto godliness.", insight:"Refuse profane and old wives' fables; not every story deserves your attention."},
{q:"Unto what should Timothy exercise himself rather?", options:["Unto godliness","Unto riches","Unto fame","Unto strength"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:7",verse:"But refuse profane and old wives’ fables, and exercise thyself rather unto godliness.", insight:"Exercise thyself unto godliness; train the soul the way athletes train the body."},
{q:"What does bodily exercise profit?", options:["Little","Nothing at all","Everything","More than godliness"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:8",verse:"For bodily exercise profiteth little: but godliness is profitable unto all things, having promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come.", insight:"Bodily exercise profiteth little, not nothing; the body matters, but godliness matters more."},
{q:"What promise does godliness carry?", options:["The life that now is, and of that which is to come","Long life only","Wealth only","Health only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:8",verse:"For bodily exercise profiteth little: but godliness is profitable unto all things, having promise of the life that now is, and of that which is to come.", insight:"Godliness has promise of this life and the next; it is the only investment that pays in both worlds."},
{q:"How does Paul describe this saying?", options:["A faithful saying and worthy of all acceptation","A hard saying","A secret saying","A doubtful saying"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:9",verse:"This is a faithful saying and worthy of all acceptation.", insight:"A faithful saying, worthy of all acceptation; some truths deserve everyone's full embrace."},
{q:"Why do we labour and suffer reproach?", options:["Because we trust in the living God","Because we seek riches","Because we love suffering","Because we fear men"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:10",verse:"For therefore we both labour and suffer reproach, because we trust in the living God, who is the Saviour of all men, specially of those that believe.", insight:"We trust in the living God, the Saviour of all men; a living God is worth a laboring life."},
{q:"What should Timothy do with these things?", options:["Command and teach them","Hide them away","Write them in secret","Tell only the elders"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:11",verse:"These things command and teach.", insight:"These things command and teach; truth kept silent helps no one."},
{q:"What should no man do regarding Timothy's youth?", options:["Despise it","Praise it","Ignore it","Envy it"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:12",verse:"Let no man despise thy youth; but be thou an example of the believers, in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.", insight:"Let no man despise thy youth; God measures ministers by maturity of character, not years."},
{q:"In what six things should Timothy be an example?", options:["In word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity","In wealth, in power, in fame, in strength, in wisdom, in age","In silence, in fasting, in travel, in building, in planting, in trading","In dreams, in visions, in signs, in wonders, in tongues, in songs"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:12",verse:"Let no man despise thy youth; but be thou an example of the believers, in word, in conversation, in charity, in spirit, in faith, in purity.", insight:"An example in word, conversation, charity, spirit, faith, purity; youth is no excuse when the pattern is this clear."},
{q:"To what three things should Timothy give attendance till Paul comes?", options:["To reading, to exhortation, to doctrine","To building, to planting, to trading","To singing, to dancing, to feasting","To traveling, to writing, to resting"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:13",verse:"Till I come, give attendance to reading, to exhortation, to doctrine.", insight:"Reading, exhortation, doctrine; the public ministry stands on these three legs."},
{q:"How was Timothy's gift given?", options:["By prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the presbytery","By purchase","By inheritance","By lot"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:14",verse:"Neglect not the gift that is in thee, which was given thee by prophecy, with the laying on of the hands of the presbytery.", insight:"Given by prophecy with the laying on of hands; gifts are recognized and commissioned by the church."},
{q:"What will appear to all if Timothy gives himself wholly to these things?", options:["Thy profiting","Thy wealth","Thy pride","Thy sorrows"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:15",verse:"Meditate upon these things; give thyself wholly to them; that thy profiting may appear to all.", insight:"That thy profiting may appear to all; growth that is real eventually shows."},
{q:"Whom will Timothy save by taking heed to himself and the doctrine?", options:["Both himself, and them that hear him","Only himself","Only the elders","The whole city"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 4:16",verse:"Take heed unto thyself, and unto the doctrine; continue in them: for in doing this thou shalt both save thyself, and them that hear thee.", insight:"Thou shalt both save thyself, and them that hear thee; a watched life and a guarded doctrine rescue two souls at once."}
]},
{n:5, name:"Widows and Elders",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 5:8",verse:"But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel."},
 facts:["Did you know the early church kept an official roll of widows it supported, and the entry requirements were strict: over sixty, the wife of one man, and well reported for good works (1 Timothy 5:9-10)?","Did you know 'worse than an infidel' is one of Paul's strongest rebukes, aimed not at outsiders but at believers who neglect their own families (1 Timothy 5:8)?","Did you know Paul's only recorded medical advice is personal and practical: 'use a little wine for thy stomach's sake and thine often infirmities,' written to Timothy himself (1 Timothy 5:23)?"],
 questions:[
{q:"How should Timothy treat an elder?", options:["Intreat him as a father","Rebuke him sharply","Ignore him","Command him"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:1",verse:"Rebuke not an elder, but intreat him as a father; and the younger men as brethren;", insight:"Intreat him as a father; correction wrapped in honor is heard, but rebuke wrapped in contempt is not."},
{q:"How should he treat the younger men?", options:["As brethren","As servants","As strangers","As rivals"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:1",verse:"Rebuke not an elder, but intreat him as a father; and the younger men as brethren;", insight:"The younger men as brethren; the church is a family before it is an organization."},
{q:"How should he treat the younger women?", options:["As sisters, with all purity","As servants","With flattery","With distance only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:2",verse:"The elder women as mothers; the younger as sisters, with all purity.", insight:"As sisters, with all purity; ministry to women guards both their honor and yours."},
{q:"Whom should the church honour?", options:["Widows that are widows indeed","The rich only","The rulers only","The young only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:3",verse:"Honour widows that are widows indeed.", insight:"Honour widows that are widows indeed; God notices the overlooked, and so must His church."},
{q:"What should children and nephews learn first?", options:["To shew piety at home, and to requite their parents","To seek riches","To leave home early","To forget the past"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:4",verse:"But if any widow have children or nephews, let them learn first to shew piety at home, and to requite their parents: for that is good and acceptable before God.", insight:"Piety begins at home, repaying parents; charity that skips the family is not charity."},
{q:"What does the widow indeed do night and day?", options:["Continueth in supplications and prayers","Sleeps soundly","Wanders the streets","Complains to all"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:5",verse:"Now she that is a widow indeed, and desolate, trusteth in God, and continueth in supplications and prayers night and day.", insight:"She trusteth in God and continueth in prayers night and day; desolation drove her to devotion."},
{q:"Who is dead while she lives?", options:["She that liveth in pleasure","She that prays daily","She that works hard","She that gives alms"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:6",verse:"But she that liveth in pleasure is dead while she liveth.", insight:"She that liveth in pleasure is dead while she liveth; a life of indulgence is a living burial."},
{q:"What is a man who provides not for his own house?", options:["Worse than an infidel","Wiser than all","Excused by poverty","Free from duty"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:8",verse:"But if any provide not for his own, and specially for those of his own house, he hath denied the faith, and is worse than an infidel.", insight:"Worse than an infidel; even unbelievers provide for their own, so a believer who does not sinks below them."},
{q:"How old must a widow be to be taken into the number?", options:["Threescore years old","Forty years old","Fifty years old","Thirty years old"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:9",verse:"Let not a widow be taken into the number under threescore years old, having been the wife of one man,", insight:"Under threescore years old she was not enrolled; the church's support went to those least able to provide for themselves."},
{q:"Which good work marks the enrolled widow?", options:["She hath washed the saints' feet","She hath built a temple","She hath written books","She hath traveled far"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:10",verse:"Well reported of for good works; if she have brought up children, if she have lodged strangers, if she have washed the saints’ feet, if she have relieved the afflicted, if she have diligently followed every good work.", insight:"Washing the saints' feet; the widow's record is written in humble, hidden service."},
{q:"What do the younger widows learn, wandering from house to house?", options:["To be idle, tattlers also and busybodies","To preach boldly","To sing sweetly","To weave finely"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:13",verse:"And withal they learn to be idle, wandering about from house to house; and not only idle, but tattlers also and busybodies, speaking things which they ought not.", insight:"Idle, tattlers, busybodies; empty hours fill with empty talk."},
{q:"What does Paul will for the younger women?", options:["Marry, bear children, guide the house","Remain idle","Travel abroad","Avoid all work"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:14",verse:"I will therefore that the younger women marry, bear children, guide the house, give none occasion to the adversary to speak reproachfully.", insight:"Marry, bear children, guide the house; Paul gives restless energy a worthy direction."},
{q:"Who should relieve believing widows?", options:["Any man or woman that believeth, having widows","The Romans","The priests only","No one"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:16",verse:"If any man or woman that believeth have widows, let them relieve them, and let not the church be charged; that it may relieve them that are widows indeed.", insight:"Let not the church be charged; families care for their own so the church can care for the truly desolate."},
{q:"Who is worthy of double honour?", options:["The elders that rule well, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine","The rich","The young","The travelers"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:17",verse:"Let the elders that rule well be counted worthy of double honour, especially they who labour in the word and doctrine.", insight:"Double honour for elders who rule well, especially preachers; those who feed the flock deserve the flock's support."},
{q:"What does Scripture say about the ox?", options:["Thou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out the corn","Sell the ox","Kill the ox","Free the ox"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:18",verse:"For the scripture saith, Thou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out the corn. And, The labourer is worthy of his reward.", insight:"Thou shalt not muzzle the ox; if God cares that working animals eat, how much more His working servants?"},
{q:"What is the labourer worthy of?", options:["His reward","Punishment","Nothing","Scorn"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:18",verse:"For the scripture saith, Thou shalt not muzzle the ox that treadeth out the corn. And, The labourer is worthy of his reward.", insight:"The labourer is worthy of his reward; ministry is work, and work deserves wages."},
{q:"How many witnesses are needed to receive an accusation against an elder?", options:["Two or three","One","Five","Ten"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:19",verse:"Against an elder receive not an accusation, but before two or three witnesses.", insight:"Not an accusation but before two or three witnesses; leaders are protected from lone, unproven charges."},
{q:"Why rebuke them that sin before all?", options:["That others also may fear","To shame them forever","To entertain the crowd","To gain followers"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:20",verse:"Them that sin rebuke before all, that others also may fear.", insight:"That others also may fear; public sin needs public correction, for the sake of all who watch."},
{q:"Before whom does Paul charge Timothy to act without partiality?", options:["Before God, the Lord Jesus Christ, and the elect angels","Before Caesar","Before the crowd","Before the priests"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:21",verse:"I charge thee before God, and the Lord Jesus Christ, and the elect angels, that thou observe these things without preferring one before another, doing nothing by partiality.", insight:"Before God, Christ, and the elect angels; heaven itself watches how we judge."},
{q:"What does Paul advise Timothy for his stomach's sake?", options:["Use a little wine for his often infirmities","Drink only water","Fast for forty days","Avoid all drink"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 5:23",verse:"Drink no longer water, but use a little wine for thy stomach’s sake and thine often infirmities.", insight:"Use a little wine for thy stomach's sake; Paul cared for Timothy's body as well as his soul."}
]},
{n:6, name:"Godliness With Contentment",
 seal:{ref:"1 Timothy 6:6",verse:"But godliness with contentment is great gain."},
 facts:["Did you know the Bible never says money is the root of all evil? Paul says 'the love of money is the root of all evil,' a warning about the heart, not the coin (1 Timothy 6:10).","Did you know 'man of God' is a rare title in Scripture? Paul gives it to Timothy here, placing him in the company of Moses and Elijah (1 Timothy 6:11).","Did you know 'the King of kings, and Lord of lords' was a title claimed by Persian and Roman rulers? Paul applies it to God alone (1 Timothy 6:15)."],
 questions:[
{q:"Why should servants count their masters worthy of all honour?", options:["That the name of God and his doctrine be not blasphemed","That they may be freed","That they may be paid more","That they may rest"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:1",verse:"Let as many servants as are under the yoke count their own masters worthy of all honour, that the name of God and his doctrine be not blasphemed.", insight:"That the name of God be not blasphemed; a servant's conduct preaches to his master."},
{q:"How should servants treat believing masters?", options:["Rather do them service, because they are brethren","Despise them","Serve them less","Leave them"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:2",verse:"And they that have believing masters, let them not despise them, because they are brethren; but rather do them service, because they are faithful and beloved, partakers of the benefit. These things teach and exhort.", insight:"Rather do them service; shared faith is a reason for better work, not lighter work."},
{q:"What marks the man who teaches otherwise?", options:["He consents not to wholesome words, even the words of our Lord Jesus Christ","He speaks too loudly","He writes too much","He travels too far"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:3",verse:"If any man teach otherwise, and consent not to wholesome words, even the words of our Lord Jesus Christ, and to the doctrine which is according to godliness;", insight:"Consent not to wholesome words; the first mark of false teaching is distance from Christ's own words."},
{q:"What is the proud teacher described as knowing?", options:["Nothing","Everything","The law","The prophets"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:4",verse:"He is proud, knowing nothing, but doting about questions and strifes of words, whereof cometh envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings,", insight:"He is proud, knowing nothing; the loudest disputants often know the least."},
{q:"What comes from strifes of words?", options:["Envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings","Peace and unity","Wisdom and joy","Faith and love"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:4",verse:"He is proud, knowing nothing, but doting about questions and strifes of words, whereof cometh envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings,", insight:"Envy, strife, railings, evil surmisings; word battles never end in love."},
{q:"What do corrupt-minded men suppose?", options:["That gain is godliness","That godliness is gain","That poverty is sin","That wealth is evil"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:5",verse:"Perverse disputings of men of corrupt minds, and destitute of the truth, supposing that gain is godliness: from such withdraw thyself.", insight:"Supposing that gain is godliness; when profit becomes the proof of piety, truth has left the building."},
{q:"What is great gain?", options:["Godliness with contentment","Riches with power","Fame with honor","Strength with beauty"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:6",verse:"But godliness with contentment is great gain.", insight:"Godliness with contentment is great gain; the richest man is the one who needs the least."},
{q:"With what should we be content?", options:["Having food and raiment","Having gold and silver","Having houses and lands","Having fame and praise"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:8",verse:"And having food and raiment let us be therewith content.", insight:"Food and raiment; Paul shrinks the list of needs to what truly sustains life."},
{q:"What happens to those who will be rich?", options:["They fall into temptation and a snare","They find true happiness","They gain eternal life","They help all men"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:9",verse:"But they that will be rich fall into temptation and a snare, and into many foolish and hurtful lusts, which drown men in destruction and perdition.", insight:"Temptation and a snare; the desire for riches is a trap with many teeth."},
{q:"What is the root of all evil?", options:["The love of money","Money itself","The poor","The rich"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:10",verse:"For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows.", insight:"The love of money, not money; the root is in the heart's affection, not the coin."},
{q:"What happened to some who coveted after money?", options:["They erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows","They became wise","They found peace","They were honored"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:10",verse:"For the love of money is the root of all evil: which while some coveted after, they have erred from the faith, and pierced themselves through with many sorrows.", insight:"Pierced themselves through with many sorrows; covetousness wounds the coveter first."},
{q:"After what six things should the man of God follow?", options:["Righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness","Gold, silver, lands, houses, fame, power","Ease, comfort, pleasure, rest, sleep, wealth","Debate, strife, envy, pride, greed, anger"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:11",verse:"But thou, O man of God, flee these things; and follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness.", insight:"Follow after righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patience, meekness; flee one thing, pursue six."},
{q:"What fight does Paul call Timothy to fight?", options:["The good fight of faith","The fight for riches","The fight for honor","The fight for power"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:12",verse:"Fight the good fight of faith, lay hold on eternal life, whereunto thou art also called, and hast professed a good profession before many witnesses.", insight:"Fight the good fight of faith; the Christian life is a contest, and faith is the field."},
{q:"Before whom did Christ Jesus witness a good confession?", options:["Before Pontius Pilate","Before Caesar","Before Herod","Before the high priest"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:13",verse:"I give thee charge in the sight of God, who quickeneth all things, and before Christ Jesus, who before Pontius Pilate witnessed a good confession;", insight:"Before Pontius Pilate He witnessed a good confession; Christ's courage before rulers is Timothy's pattern."},
{q:"How long should Timothy keep the commandment without spot?", options:["Until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ","For one year","Until Paul returns","For forty days"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:14",verse:"That thou keep this commandment without spot, unrebukeable, until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ:", insight:"Until the appearing of our Lord; faithfulness has no retirement date."},
{q:"How is God described in Paul's charge?", options:["The blessed and only Potentate, the King of kings, and Lord of lords","A distant observer","A harsh judge only","A silent watcher"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:15",verse:"Which in his times he shall shew, who is the blessed and only Potentate, the King of kings, and Lord of lords;", insight:"King of kings, and Lord of lords; every earthly throne answers to this one."},
{q:"In what does God dwell?", options:["In the light which no man can approach unto","In darkness","In the temple only","In the heavens only"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:16",verse:"Who only hath immortality, dwelling in the light which no man can approach unto; whom no man hath seen, nor can see: to whom be honour and power everlasting. Amen.", insight:"Dwelling in unapproachable light; God is near in grace, yet beyond our sight in glory."},
{q:"In what should the rich not trust?", options:["In uncertain riches","In the living God","In good works","In prayer"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:17",verse:"Charge them that are rich in this world, that they be not highminded, nor trust in uncertain riches, but in the living God, who giveth us richly all things to enjoy;", insight:"Nor trust in uncertain riches; wealth is a poor foundation because it moves."},
{q:"In what should the rich be rich?", options:["In good works","In gold","In lands","In jewels"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:18",verse:"That they do good, that they be rich in good works, ready to distribute, willing to communicate;", insight:"Rich in good works, ready to distribute; true wealth is measured by what flows out."},
{q:"What should Timothy avoid?", options:["Profane and vain babblings, and oppositions of science falsely so called","The reading of Scripture","Prayer and fasting","The company of believers"], a:0, ref:"1 Timothy 6:20",verse:"O Timothy, keep that which is committed to thy trust, avoiding profane and vain babblings, and oppositions of science falsely so called:", insight:"Avoid profane and vain babblings; not all knowledge deserves the name, and some of it steals faith."}
]}
]);
